---
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import SEO from "../components/SEO.astro";
---

<!doctype html>

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />

    <meta
      name="viewport"
      content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"
    />

    <SEO
      title="The Problem of Tolerance | Minimal Tolerance"
      description="The moral and economic foundations of the Theory of Minimal Tolerance"
      lang="en"
    />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Header />

    <main>

      <!-- PAGE TITLE -->

      <section class="page-hero">

        <div class="container">

          <p class="eyebrow">
            THE THEORY
          </p>

          <h1>
            The problem of tolerance
          </h1>
        </div>

      </section>

      <!-- OPENING STATEMENT -->

      <section class="statement">

        <blockquote>
          The State does not become legitimate.
          At most, it may be tolerated
        </blockquote>

      </section>

      <!-- MORAL PROBLEM -->

      <section class="content-section">

        <div class="container">

          <p class="eyebrow">
            THE MORAL PROBLEM
          </p>

          <h2>
            Political authority rests on coercion
          </h2>

          <p>
           The State is routinely exempted from principles that govern every other human relationship. Consent is treated as essential in private interactions but becomes secondary once political authority enters the picture
          </p>

          <p>
          Unlike private relationships, the relationship between the individual and the State is neither voluntary nor optional. The ability to impose a decision does not establish a moral right to impose it. Compliance and legitimacy are not the same thing
          </p>


        </div>

      </section>

      <!-- EFFICIENCY PROBLEM -->

      <section class="content-section content-section-alt">

        <div class="container">

          <p class="eyebrow">
            THE EFFICIENCY PROBLEM
          </p>

          <h2>
            Why centralized decisions fail
          </h2>

          <p>
      Complex societies depend on dispersed knowledge, market prices, entrepreneurial discovery, and competitive feedback. No central authority can fully replicate the processes through which decentralized decisions coordinate human activity
          </p>

          <p>
      The result is not merely less information, but weaker adaptation, slower error correction, and less efficient allocation of scarce resources
          </p>



        </div>

      </section>

      <!-- MARKET FAILURE -->

      <section class="content-section">

        <div class="container">

          <p class="eyebrow">
            MARKET FAILURE
          </p>

          <h2>
            The existence of a problem does not establish the case for coercion
          </h2>

          <p>
        Imperfect outcomes are often treated as automatic justification for state action. The diagnosis quickly becomes a prescription, as though identifying a difficulty were enough to validate the proposed remedy
          </p>

          <p>
   Markets are judged against reality, while governments are judged against aspiration. Political decision-making does not eliminate the underlying problems. It merely changes their form
          </p>

      

        </div>

      </section>

      <!-- STRUCTURAL FREE RIDER -->

      <section class="content-section content-section-alt">

        <div class="container">

          <p class="eyebrow">
            NOT A MARKET FAILURE
          </p>

          <h2>
            A structural free-rider problem
          </h2>

          <p>
           The phenomenon addressed here is not a market failure in the classical sense. It is a structural free-rider problem: when exclusion is difficult or impossible, individuals may benefit regardless of whether they contribute, weakening the incentives for voluntary provision
          </p>

          <p>
          The relevant case is narrower than the standard market-failure argument. It concerns situations in which voluntary provision collapses persistently because of the incentive structure itself. The objection is not moral, but operational
          </p>

        </div>

      </section>

      <!-- LINK TO THEOREM -->

      <section class="content-section">

        <div class="container">

          <p class="eyebrow">
            THE THEOREM
          </p>

          <h2>
            Defining the boundary
          </h2>

          <div class="buttons">

            <a href="/theorem/" class="primary">
              Read the Theorem
            </a>

          </div>

        </div>

      </section>

    </main>

    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>

